1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,700
Are you a hopeless romantic?
你是一个无可救药的浪漫主义者吗？

2
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:08,350
Do you get excited by the idea of falling in love with the man or woman of your dreams?
你会不会因为爱上梦中情人的想法而兴奋不已？

3
00:00:08,350 --> 00:00:14,080
Does watching a romcom or a period drama on TV make you cry?
在电视上看一部浪漫爱情喜剧或年代剧会不会让你落泪呢？

4
00:00:14,080 --> 00:00:21,610
Or are you someone who thinks romance is based on an idealised view of reality that doesn't really exist?
又或者，你是否认为浪漫是基于一种并不存在的理想化的现实？

5
00:00:21,610 --> 00:00:24,010
Well, you could be right!
嗯，你也许是对的！

6
00:00:24,010 --> 00:00:32,980
I'm no romantic and more of a realist - taking a more practical approach to love - maybe that's why I never had many girlfriends!
我不是一个浪漫主义者，更多的是一个现实主义者——对爱情采取更现实的态度——也许这就是为什么我从来没有过很多女朋友！

7
00:00:32,980 --> 00:00:39,930
But if you really are expecting the kind of love that makes your heart flutter with happiness you may be disappointed.
但是如果你真的期待那种能让你的心因幸福而怦怦直跳的爱情，你可能会失望的。

8
00:00:39,930 --> 00:00:54,740
Our expectation of what romance is, probably originated from the Romantic period - an artistic, literary, musical and intellectual movement that originated in Europe in the 18th Century.
我们对浪漫的期待，很可能源自浪漫主义时期——这是一场18世纪起源于欧洲的艺术、文学、音乐和思想运动。

9
00:00:54,740 --> 00:01:04,890
The notion of falling in love, getting married and living happily ever after were culturally held ideas formed during this period and still exist today.
坠入爱河、结婚并从此过上幸福生活的观念是在这个时期形成的文化观念，并且一直延续到今天。

10
00:01:04,890 --> 00:01:16,060
Of course there's nothing wrong with liking the idea of romance; it makes us feel good — but we must be careful not to use it as a benchmark for our own relationships.
当然，喜欢浪漫的想法并没有错；它让我们感觉良好——但我们必须小心，不要把它当作我们自己恋爱关系的衡量基准。

11
00:01:16,060 --> 00:01:21,830
This idealised version of love leaves out the nitty-gritty of real-life relationships.
这种理想化的爱情版本忽略了现实生活中恋爱关系的具体细节。

12
00:01:21,830 --> 00:01:26,920
There's usually work, finances and other stresses of everyday life to deal with.
通常还有工作、财务以及日常生活中的其他压力需要处理。

13
00:01:26,920 --> 00:01:33,610
You can't expect romantic gestures like a bunch of red roses every day — there are bills to pay!
你不能指望每天都有像一束红玫瑰这样的浪漫举动——毕竟还有账单要付呢！

14
00:01:33,610 --> 00:01:40,020
Many popular love stories end at the point where the characters get together or tie the knot.
许多流行的爱情故事都在男女主角在一起或结婚的时候就结束了。

15
00:01:40,020 --> 00:01:43,810
That puts the focus on finding someone special.
这把重点放在了找到那个特别的人上。

16
00:01:43,810 --> 00:01:49,740
But very few show us how to keep that perfect catch over a long period of time.
但很少有故事向我们展示如何在很长一段时间内留住那个理想伴侣。

17
00:01:49,740 --> 00:01:52,250
A relationship is hard work.
维持一段恋爱关系是很不容易的。

18
00:01:52,250 --> 00:01:56,750
It's frustrating, messy and can be emotionally damaging.
它会让人沮丧、混乱，而且可能会在情感上造成伤害。

19
00:01:56,750 --> 00:02:02,520
If you're still convinced romance isn't dead, how will you ever find it?
如果你仍然坚信浪漫并没有消亡，那你要如何才能找到它呢？

20
00:02:02,520 --> 00:02:13,760
Historian and TV presenter Lucy Worsley thinks nowadays, any idea of romance is dying because it has become "too easy" to meet new people via dating apps.
历史学家兼电视节目主持人露西·沃斯利认为，如今，任何关于浪漫的概念都在逐渐消逝，因为通过约会应用程序结识新人变得“太容易”了。

21
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:23,810
She told the BBC the "slow exquisite torture of love in Jane Austen novels no longer existed in the age of Grindr and Tinder [apps]."
她告诉英国广播公司：“简·奥斯汀小说中那种缓慢而美妙的爱情煎熬在Grindr和Tinder（约会应用程序）时代已经不复存在了。”

22
00:02:23,810 --> 00:02:38,370
Perhaps romance is best left to the movies - a fantasy that makes us feel good - and instead concentrate on finding a good and healthy relationship, with all its ups and downs, but with lots of love.
也许浪漫最好还是留在电影里——那是一种能让我们感觉良好的幻想——而我们应该专注于寻找一段美好且健康的恋爱关系，这段关系起起伏伏，但却充满了爱。

23
00:02:38,370 --> 00:02:40,790
Do you believe romance is really dead?
你相信浪漫真的存在吗？
